; 05-rewrite-with-equality.kurt
;
; Rewrite with equality
;
; Load `equality` for reflexivity and Leibniz substitution. If `a = b` and a fact contains `a`,
; Kurt can replace that occurrence with `b`.

load equality
const a, b, f

use a = b
use f(a) = a
f(b) = a

; The last step uses the labelled rule `equal-elim` from `equality.kurt`. Equality is symmetric,
; so Kurt can also use an equation in the other direction.
;
; When a rewrite does not happen, state an intermediate expression showing exactly one
; replacement. Kurt is a one-step checker, not a multi-step simplifier.
;
; A longer derivation chains several such one-step rewrites, each line continuing from the
; previous line's right-hand side instead of restating it -- see keywords/33-chain.kurt for how
; `chain` works and which operators may be mixed this way:

f(a) = a
     = b
f(a) = b
;
; A rule with conditions works for rewriting too, if the conditions are facts: with
; `$a ∈ K ⇒ $a · 1 = $a` and `b ∈ K`, the step from `c · (b · 1) = a` to `c · b = a` is
; accepted -- Kurt derives the instance `b · 1 = b` and shows it as a step of its own (line `33a`
; before line 33):

load set
const K, c
infix · 70 70                   ; `·` is written between its arguments (lesson 27)
use $x ∈ K  ⇒  $x · 1 = $x      "one-right"
use b ∈ K
use c · (b · 1) = a
c · b = a

; A chain can mix the two kinds of rewriting from this lesson -- plain equality and a conditional
; rule -- in one derivation, as long as each link is still a single step:

c · (b · 1) = a
            = f(a)
            = f(b)
